{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"63e6ad90-b6dc-4770-ba86-e9976238833c","step":{"id":"s1","script":"Exponential change multiplies by the same factor each time. These two quantities both start at eight.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"model","region":"A1","latex":"Q(t)=Q_0 b^t"},{"op":"text","id":"factor","anchor":{"el":"model","side":"below","gap":0.06},"content":"b = multiplier per time unit"},{"op":"axes","id":"axes","region":"right","x":{"min":0,"max":4,"label":"time t"},"y":{"min":0,"max":34,"label":"quantity Q"}}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"63e6ad90-b6dc-4770-ba86-e9976238833c","step":{"id":"s2","script":"With a multiplier of two, the quantity doubles each time: eight, sixteen, then thirty-two. The curve becomes progressively steeper.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"growth","anchor":{"el":"factor","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"G(t)=8\\cdot 2^t"},{"op":"curve","id":"growthcurve","axes_id":"axes","expr":"8*2^x","domain":[0,2]},{"op":"equation","id":"growthvalues","anchor":{"el":"growth","side":"below","gap":0.04},"latex":"G(0)=8,\\quad G(1)=16,\\quad G(2)=32"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"63e6ad90-b6dc-4770-ba86-e9976238833c","step":{"id":"s3","script":"With a multiplier of one-half, the quantity halves each time. It approaches zero but never reaches zero in this model.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"decay","anchor":{"el":"growthvalues","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"D(t)=8\\left(\\frac12\\right)^t"},{"op":"curve","id":"decaycurve","axes_id":"axes","expr":"8*(0.5)^x","domain":[0,4]},{"op":"equation","id":"decayvalues","anchor":{"el":"decay","side":"below","gap":0.04},"latex":"D(0)=8,\\quad D(1)=4,\\quad D(2)=2"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"If the quantity keeps 80% of its value each hour, what is its multiplier, and is this growth or decay?","expected_gist":"The multiplier is 0.8; this is exponential decay."}}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"63e6ad90-b6dc-4770-ba86-e9976238833c","step":{"id":"s4","script":"For positive starting quantities, multipliers above one mean growth; between zero and one mean decay. Percentage changes become multipliers, not fixed additions.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"rule","anchor":{"el":"decayvalues","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"b>1:\\ \\text{growth}\\qquad 0<b<1:\\ \\text{decay}"},{"op":"equation","id":"percent","anchor":{"el":"rule","side":"below","gap":0.05},"latex":"+20\\%\\Rightarrow b=1.20\\qquad -20\\%\\Rightarrow b=0.80"}],"checkpoint":null}}
